ecm: put the egress tag_8021q VID in NSS rules so routed frames deliver precisely

ECM resolves a DSA user port (or a bridge over one) to its bare GMAC conduit
(patch 0026), so routed rules carry no VLAN and the NSS firmware emits the
forwarded frames UNTAGGED on the conduit. The AN8855 classifies untagged CPU
ingress into the CPU port's PVID - pinned to the all-ports VID 0 - and FLOODS.
That delivers (it is what the 895 Mbit/s-era builds relied on implicitly), but
switch flood replication is gated by the slowest member port: a 100 Mb/s
device on the LAN caps every routed flow near its own link speed (measured:
~60 Mbit/s TCP with such a port in the flood set, 860 Mbit/s without).

Instead, put each side's tag_8021q VID into vlan_primary_rule: the 'to' side
VID as egress_vlan_tag (flow-direction egress) and the 'from' side VID as
ingress_vlan_tag (return-direction egress; it also matches the tag the CPU
port itself adds on ingress frames, since the CPU port's per-VID egress
tagging is TAGGED for every tag_8021q VID). The switch then ARL-forwards
within that VID to exactly the learned port - verified by raw-frame injection:
bridge-VID-tagged frames deliver 5/5 with zero flood leak.

The VID is taken from each side's INNERMOST hierarchy element
([x_ifaces_first]) - the same element whose AE number became the rule's
flow/return interface number - which in every DSA-backed topology is the DSA
user port itself (ecm_interface_heirarchy_construct() descends br-lan/lanN,
br-wan/lanN and pppoe-wan -> br-wan -> lanN all the way to the port). Outer
elements are never consulted: a bridge at the top of the list does not imply
the flow egresses a DSA port (it may egress a Wi-Fi member of the same
bridge). The helper additionally refuses to yield a VID when the switch does
not run a tag_8021q tagger (the VID would be meaningless to e.g. qca8k) and
when the port's bridge is VLAN-aware (delivery then follows the bridge's real
VLANs; the vsc73xx tagger's own xmit refuses the bridge VID in that case
too, and the port's PVID is the bridge PVID so NSS-ingress frames would not
carry the tag_8021q VID either).

Routed connections only; a rule already claimed by a real VLAN interface (or
the bridged passthrough) is left alone. VID 0 doubles as "not DSA-backed", so
non-DSA topologies are untouched.

Gate per side, not both-sides-combined: when the L3 interface is itself a VLAN
netdev (br-lan.1 over a vlan_filtering bridge - the standard OpenWrt shape),
the stock VLAN branch legitimately claims that side of the rule, and a
combined gate then skips the DSA side entirely, leaving its egress on the
flood path. Measured on the target: combined gate = 55 Mbit/s TCP (flood,
gated by a 100 Mb/s member port); per-side gate = 940 Mbit/s TCP at 98.9%
idle, 14 retransmits, zero flood leak.

--- a/frontends/nss/ecm_nss_common.h
+++ b/frontends/nss/ecm_nss_common.h
@@ -114,6 +114,72 @@
 	return dev;
 }
 
+#if IS_ENABLED(CONFIG_NET_DSA)
+#include <linux/dsa/8021q.h>
+#include <linux/if_bridge.h>
+#endif
+
+/*
+ * ecm_nss_common_dsa_8021q_vid_get()
+ *	The tag_8021q VID that delivers a frame PRECISELY to `dev` when dev is
+ *	a DSA user port whose switch runs a tag_8021q tagger:
+ *
+ *	  - a bridged DSA user port  -> its bridge's tag_8021q VID (the switch
+ *	    then ARL-forwards within that VID to the learned port), but only
+ *	    while the bridge is NOT VLAN-aware: a VLAN-aware bridge delivers by
+ *	    its real VLANs and the tagger itself refuses the bridge VID then
+ *	    (see vsc73xx_xmit());
+ *	  - a standalone DSA user port -> its standalone VID (members: the port
+ *	    and its CPU conduit only).
+ *
+ *	Returns 0 (never a tag_8021q VID) when dev is not a DSA user port, the
+ *	switch does not run a tag_8021q tagger, or the bridge is VLAN-aware -
+ *	callers use the value as both VID and found-flag. Bridge masters are
+ *	deliberately NOT resolved here: a bridge above the interface list does
+ *	not imply the flow egresses a DSA port (it may egress a Wi-Fi or other
+ *	non-DSA member of the same bridge), so only the port itself is ever a
+ *	valid anchor.
+ */
+static inline uint16_t ecm_nss_common_dsa_8021q_vid_get(struct net_device *dev)
+{
+#if IS_ENABLED(CONFIG_NET_DSA)
+	struct dsa_port *dp;
+	struct net_device *br;
+
+	if (!dsa_user_dev_check(dev)) {
+		return 0;
+	}
+
+	dp = dsa_port_from_netdev(dev);
+	if (IS_ERR_OR_NULL(dp)) {
+		return 0;
+	}
+
+	/*
+	 * Only a tag_8021q tagger delivers by these VIDs. On any other tag
+	 * protocol (e.g. qca8k) the switch knows nothing of them and the
+	 * inserted tag would break delivery outright.
+	 */
+	if (!dp->ds->tag_8021q_ctx) {
+		return 0;
+	}
+
+	br = dsa_port_bridge_dev_get(dp);
+	if (br) {
+		unsigned int bridge_num = dsa_port_bridge_num_get(dp);
+
+		if (!bridge_num || br_vlan_enabled(br)) {
+			return 0;
+		}
+		return dsa_tag_8021q_bridge_vid(bridge_num);
+	}
+
+	return dsa_tag_8021q_standalone_vid(dp);
+#else
+	return 0;
+#endif
+}
+
